  "correct": "E",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Use prime bases",
      "body": "<p>Write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>125</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>5</mn><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>. Then gather powers of 2 and of 5 separately.</p>",
      "recap": "Unique prime factors expose whether a denominator can cancel.",
      "pitfall": "Raise every factor to the whole exponent: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.",
      "pause": "Rewrite numerator and denominator using only the prime bases 2 and 5.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-prime-factorisation",
          "reminder": "Distinct prime factors identify which numerator and denominator factors can cancel."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Collect the prime exponents",
      "body": "Gather the exponent of each prime separately, adding numerator exponents and subtracting denominator exponents. Simplify the expressions in both parameters.",
      "recap": "A product of distinct prime powers with integer exponents is an integer exactly when all its exponents are nonnegative.",
      "pitfall": "A power of one prime cannot cancel a denominator power of a different prime.",
      "pause": "Calculate the two prime exponents, impose integrality and apply the given nonzero parameter conditions.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-prime-factorisation",
          "reminder": "A product of distinct prime powers with integer exponents is an integer exactly when all its exponents are nonnegative."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>Prime factorisation gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mn>20</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>8</mn><mi>c</mi></msup><msup><mn>125</mn><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>c</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mn>5</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mn>5</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</p><p>Since 2 and 5 are distinct primes and the exponents are integers, this is an integer exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Negative exponents would leave uncancellable prime factors in a denominator.</p><p>The given nonzero restrictions turn these into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: <strong>E.</strong></p>",
